Object initialization syntax and grammar draft
==============================================

Constructor syntaxes
--------------------

Additionally to the `init` methods that object can define, I wanted to have a special way to simply attribute values to members directly at allocation. To avoid writing trivial `init`s, which take arguments and assign them directly to members, I though of this kind of syntax:

	obj = Object with
		member1 = value1,
		member2 = value2,
		-- ...
		memberN = valueN
	end

That would be exactly equivalent to:

	obj = Object
	obj.member1 = value1
	obj.member2 = value2
	-- ...
	obj.memberN = valueN

Then I thought about array and table constructors. I always planned on having these two particular syntaxes to build these objects directly from code, and thus be able to use sool as a data description language:

	arr = [1,3,4,5,67,2]
	tab = { "blah" : 4, "foo" : 3, meh[3] : 25.sqrt }

But two concerns arose:

-	this forces to have syntax be plugged directly to specific classes, arbitrarily chosen by me (growable array and hash table). They will be standard, sure, but what if a user happens to prefer his very own containers instead? A whole part of the language will be useless to them
-	if someone uses their own classes, since they wont have the special syntax, they will have to write all the static information as method calls:
	
		obj = MyContainer
		obj.insert(a)
		obj.insert(b)
		obj.insert(c)
	
	and this looks far less nice for data description

So what about making "generic" object constructors, that map to allocation followed by calls to setters, and that can therefore be used for any class? For instance:

	obj = Class { a : b, c : d }	-- Class must answer to "[]="

would be translated to:

	obj = Class
	obj[a] = b
	obj[c] = d

While

	obj = Class [ a, b, c ]			-- Class must answer to "<~"

would be in fact:

	obj = Class
	obj <~ a
	obj <~ b
	obj <~ c

And the two standard objects would be a simple special case: `{...}` would default to the Table, and `[...]` to the array.

Alternatively, we could forget about the special cases, and force the presence of a class name, therefore losing the shortcuts and the preference for my personal favorite containers.

So, we have four ways to initialize an object:

	-- automatic call to init()
	obj = Class
	obj = Class(...)
	
	-- automatic member setting
	obj = Class with
		member = value
	end
	
	-- automatic call to index setter []=
	obj = Class {
		index : value
	}
	
	-- automatic insertion of values with <~
	obj = Class [ val, val, val ]

Funny thing, we can also use the "table form" for arrays:

	arr = Array|text { 1 = "one", 10 = "ten" }

The growable array will automatically resize.

Would merging the member and index ones be reasonable? They do look pretty much the same, but have different meanings. `member = ` is really a call to `member=`, while `index :` is a call to `[]=` with the argument `index`. Using a different syntax is important, to distinguish the meanings. But what about:

	obj = Object {
		member = meh,
		[index] = blah
	}

That does look like `obj.member=` and `obj[index]=`, which are the actual meanings. But it does force to but brackets around all indices, which is fairly annoying, especially if there are only these. And will there be many (or any) cases where you'd need to mix the two? One is meant for object with named members, while the other is meant for containers. It's unlikely that an object will have the two. And if it does, well, it will take lines of explicit code to fill both.

And *good news everyone*, having three different opening tokens (`with`, `[` and `{`) makes the thing LL(1) again. And that is always pretty nice. Make my job easier.

Type inferring
--------------

One detail left is that the compiler will need to do type inferring, when the class name is not mentionned. Array and Table are templates only, so depending on the types of the values found in the constructor, the compiler will decide what `T` to use in `Array|T` and what `K` and `V` in `Table|(K,V)`.

It's simple when there are directly values:

	arr = [1,2,3]			-- Array|num
	tab = { "meh" : 5 }		-- Table|(text,num)

It gets more difficult when using the empty constructors for quick allocation:

	arr = []
	tab = {}

in which case the type inferring will be delayed until the first assignment.

	arr <~ "meh"	-- looks like a call to "<~(text)", must be Array|text
	tab[4] = true	-- "[]=(num,bool)", that's certainly a Table|(num,bool)

The difficulty will arrive when combining this with coercion and untyped `nil` literals. A new mission for capt'n semantics!

Also, literal arrays of compile-time known values can probably be optimized to be written directly in the machine code and copied directly into a growable array (instead of making a new array and calling `<~` many times). In fact, the growable array class will most likely have an `init(#type arr)`, to promote a simple array to growable. And it might have a "read only" flag to be sure not to write into these, and copy on write. But this is the domain of optimization. And we shall not think of this prematurely.

The syntax strikes back
-----------------------

Here is a draft of the new grammar. Followed by random comments.

	-- All "list-<R>" rules are defined as "<R> { ',' <R> }", that is, comma separated list of at least one <R>
	
	chunk :=	{ composite-def }
	
	composite-def :=	( 'struct' | 'class' | 'extend' )
						class-name
						[ ':' class-name ]
						composite-body
						'end'
	
	class-name :=	CName [ template ]
	
	template := 	'|'
					( type | '(' list-type ')' )
	
	type :=			'num' | 'bool' | 'text' | 'opaque' | 'data' |
					class-name | type-low-array | type-array | type-table
	
	left-safe-type :=	'num' | 'bool' | 'text' | 'opaque' | 'data' |
						class-name | type-low-array
	
	type-low-array :=	'#' type
	
	type-array :=		'[' type ']'
	
	type-table :=		'{' type ',' type '}'
	
	composite-body :=	{ member-def | method-def | foreign-def }
	
	member-def :=	[ 'shared' ] [ 'internal' ]
					type
					Name
	
	method-def :=	[ 'internal' ]
					( 'method' | 'selfless' )
					[ list-type ]
					method-name
					'(' [ list-arg-def ] ')'
					block
					'end'
	
	foreign-def :=	[ 'internal' ] 'foreign'
					[ type ]
					Name
					'(' [ list-arg-def ] ')'
	
	method-name :=	Name [ '=' ] |
					free-binop | 
					'[' ']' [ '=' ]
	
	free-binop :=	'+' | '-' | '*' | '/' | '%' |
					'<' | '>' | '<=' | '>=' | '^' |
					'~' | '<~'
	
	arg-def :=		type [ 'ref' ] Name
	
	block :=		{ statement }
					[ final-statement ]
	
	statement :=	do-stat |
					while-stat |
					for-stat |
					if-stat |
					assignment |
					call-stat
	
	final-statement :=	'break' | 'continue' | 'return' [ list-exp ]
	
	do-stat :=		'do' block 'end'
	
	while-stat :=	'while' exp [ 'as' Name ] 'do' block 'end'
	
	for-stat :=		'for' list-name 'in' list-exp 'do' block 'end'
	
	if-stat :=		'if' exp [ 'as' Name ] 'then' block
					{ 'elseif' exp [ 'as' Name ] 'then' block }
					[ 'else' block ]
					'end'
	
	assignment :=	list-left-value '=' list-exp
	
	call-stat :=	left-safe-exp { exp-tail }
	
	exp :=			term { binop term }
	
	binop :=		free-binop | '==' | '~=' | 'and' | 'or'
	
	term :=			unary-op | typecast | basic-exp
	
	unary-op :=		( '-' | 'not' ) term
	
	typecast :=		basic-exp 'to' class-name
	
	basic-exp :=	( left-safe-exp | array-cons | table-cons |	'(' exp ')' )
					{ exp-tail }
	
	left-safe-exp :=	atomic-exp | vec-cons | selfless-call | object-cons
	
	atomic-exp :=	Name | Number | String | 'true' | 'false' | 'nil'
	
	vec-cons :=		'<' list-basic-exp '>'
	
	array-cons :=	'[' [ list-exp ] ']'
	
	table-cons :=	'{' [ list-table-cons-pair ] '}'
	
	table-cons-pair :=	exp ':' exp
	
	object-cons :=	( class-name | type-low-array )
					[ array-cons | table-cons | with-cons | call-arg ]
	
	with-cons :=	'with' list-with-cons-pair 'end'
	
	with-cons-pair :=	Name '=' exp
	
	call-arg	:=	'(' [ list-exp ] ')'
	
	exp-tail :=		indexing | method-call
	
	indexing :=		'[' list-exp ']'
	
	method-call := 	'.' Name [ call-arg ]
	
	left-value :=		left-safe-exp { exp-tail } ( indexing | '.' Name )
	
	selfless-call :=	left-safe-type ':' Name [ call-arg ]


I thought `private` could be replaced with `internal`. That way, there won't be confusion with the usual meaning (C++/Java) which is less restrictive, and "internal" is just as relevant: can be used only from within the object.

For now, I keep `[type]` and `{type,type}` in the game. They cause a few problems regarding parsing, but I think they are important shortcuts. The problems are:
	meth [num] blah() end
	meth []=(num a) end

`[]=` is the only case when removing `op` to introduce an operator overload is problematic. No other type starts like an operator. Since it's just a matter of looking one more token ahead, I'll keep it that way.

	blah = [num](50)
	blah = [a,b,c,d]

We don't know if we start an array constructor, or an array class name. And since both are recursive, we need unbounded lookahead. That annoys me a bit more. That might require having trackback, that is parsing functions allowed to fail without blocking the whole process. That makes the parsing non linear, and therefore slower. Same with `{` at the beginning of an expression.

In fact it's even worse. The grammar is ambiguous:

	blah = [#num]

Is this a high level array constructor filled with a single low-level array constructor? Or is it the Array|#num type, used to instantiate one of them? So it seems the shortcut types won't cut it for in-code use. Too bad.

Also, another LL(1) conflict is a class name at the beginning of an expression:

	blah = Object(...)
	blah = Object:method

But there too, a simple one token lookahead will differentiate between all four ways of initializing and an explicit selfless call.

The classic (for me at least) conflict between assignment and lone method call is still present. A statement that starts with a `left-safe-exp` can be either. The hand written parser will silently left-factor the grammar and sort it out.

This `left-safe-exp` is simply there to restrict what tokens can start a statement. Because of the table and array constructors that can follow object instantiations, this would be ambiguous:

	blah = Object [meh] + 5

Is it `Object[meh]`, to which we add 5, or is it `Object`, and next statement is the operation `[meh] + 5`? A similar conflict exists for simple indexing actually:

	blah = meh [10] + 5

To avoid this, a statement cannot start in `(`, `{` or `[`. Every other expression is "left safe".

The `lvalue` rule is defined as a "first-follow" conflicting rule. But it basically means that it must end in a setter method (indexing or single name). In practice, the parser will use the same rule as the `call-stat`, and simply check whether the highest level node is an indexing or a member access, and reject as an invalid left-value otherwise.

As it is now, the `with ... end` block is part of an expression, so that would be legal:

	blah = Object with
		meh = 5
	end . lol + 5

It does look weird, but I don't see how it could be an issue.

Syntax can be annoying
----------------------

As you see, I had to define a set of "left-safe" rules, and they all come back to the same issue, caused by two things:

- an expression can end in an optional `(...)`, `[...]` or `{...}`, by means of optional arguments to a method call or optional arguments/initializer to object instantiation
- a statement can end in a expression (right value or lone call)
- a statement can begin in an expression (left value or lone call)
- some expressions start in `(` `[` or `{`

So we have a first-follow conflict (in LL(1) grammar terms), and even an ambiguity in the grammar, so my first thought was simply to restrict what expressions can start a statement (as left-value or lone expression).

But it makes me sad, because it makes the grammar heavier and it looks like it's full of exceptions.

C would solve this problem by forcing every statement to end in `;`. Similarly we could say that the new line is syntactic and ends statement, but I refuse. A few other solutions are:

- make sure that `(` `[` and `{` cannot start expressions. That would mean having no "naked" table or array constructor. I had thought about that, and imagined that even the standard contained would need to be named before initializing them: `blah = Array|num[1,2,3,4]`. It sucks a bit, but why not. More harsh is the parentheses. They are used to group terms in expressions, and they have every right to appear anywhere. So we'd need an exception and say that a statement cannot begin with `(`.
- make sure that expression cannot end in optional bracketed stuff. For the initializers, that would mean we need a token to introduce the initialization: `Class![1,2,3,4]` which is almost ok. For the parentheses, on the other hand, it's a whole different story, because that concerns the optional arguments to method calls. And this one is of paramount importance (access to members and call to methods **must** look the same, by the principle of hiding implementations). So no way it's going away. There too, the statement must be the one to fold, and can not start in a `(`.

So in any case, we cannot make everything smooth. We have to choose one exception. For the sake of following what pretty much every other language has done before, we have to keep parentheses to enclose the list of arguments to a call, and for term grouping in an expression.

Let's examine why these also start statements:

	(a + b).print
	[1,2,3,4].max.print
	{"a": meh, "b": boo}.internize	-- assume that stores in a shared member

They all consist in calling methods on temporary objects, which are lost right after the statement. That means that their only use is in the side effects of the method calls. Printing, storing internally, saving the world, whatever. It might be useful sometimes, but let's face it, you can always take one more step, and use a temporary local variable to do the same job.

What do we really need or want as statement starter then? For left-values we need at least identifiers:

	blah = meh
	blah.lol = meh
	blah[2].lol = meh

and class names, for selfless methods:

	Class:member = meh

At some point I thought having string literals could be interesting, since a global function `print` cannot exist:

	"some message".print

but is that really necessary? Any serious output will use formating functions or file descriptors, or internal logging systems like:

	out:write("some message")
	errors <~ "Derp derp"

In fact, checking any Lua or C or C++ source I ever wrote, I realize I've lived fairly well without having to start any expression-statement with anything else than a name. So maybe we could restrict to that. Left-values and lone calls must start in names (or class names for selfless calls).

That frees up a lot of things that can be optional at the end of an expression. That solves our `[]` `()` `{}` problem, and opens the possibility for other fun stuff like the Lua-like shortcut:

	blah.foo.bar "meh"		-- calling bar("meh")

In fact, that could work for any atomic expression other than a Name (but these are not so clear):

	foo 5
	meh = a.bar true

and crazier, the other Lua shortcut of taking a single table argument:

	a.take_this_table { a:b, c:d }

For the string and the table, it's fairly justified, because you'd otherwise end up with annoying double-brackets: `blah("meh")` or `foo({a:b})`. For the `[`, no choice, it can be mixed up with the indexing. No luck either for the vector literal, that starts like an operator (`<`).

The one small inconsistency that would come with having parentheses-free call with single table argument is that

	meh = ClassName { a : b }

could potentially look like either use the special "table-like" initialization (that means call `meh[a] = b`), or use the `init()` call with a single argument `{ a : b }`. However, that shortcut in Lua is meant when using the language as data description, usually to build objects on the go, based on tables. This is better done with *actual* objects from specific classes (for with the table-like syntax will fill members) instead of passing a standard table, and using it to initialize the object.

So I don't mind removing the single-table shortcut. The only one left is the literal string.

One final thing is the type shortcuts for standard arrays and tables. Since they start like array and table constructors, they cannot appear where these would appear. That is at any non-statement starting expression. And that means bringing in lots of exceptions, and that annoys me.

I have a choice: remove these shortcuts entirely (meh, I liked them), or restrict them to the only place where they won't bother anybody: in declarative places (members, and methods prototypes). Everywhere else (method name, parent method, in code instantiation, selfless method call), there must be complete normal types. Normally, these uses are few, thanks to type inferring, while type declarations are often. I'm satisfied.

A final final thing about types, we still need the primitive ones in-code as well, for access to selfless methods (`text:nilval`).

The result
----------

	-- All "list-<R>" rules are defined as "<R> { ',' <R> }", that is, comma separated list of at least one <R>

	chunk :=	{ composite-def }

	composite-def :=	( 'struct' | 'class' | 'extend' )
						class-name
						[ ':' class-name ]
						composite-body
						'end'

	class-name :=	CName [ template ]

	template := 	'|' ( type | '(' list-type ')' )

	type :=			'num' | 'bool' | 'text' | 'opaque' | 'data' |
					class-name | type-low-array

	type-low-array :=	'#' type

	type-decl :=		type | type-array-shortcut | type-table-shortcut

	type-array-shortcut :=	'[' type ']'

	type-table-shortcut :=	'{' type ',' type '}'

	composite-body :=	{ member-def | method-def | foreign-def }

	member-def :=	[ 'shared' ] [ 'internal' ]
					type-decl
					Name

	method-def :=	[ 'internal' ]
					( 'method' | 'selfless' )
					[ list-type-decl ]
					[ 'ref' ]
					method-name
					'(' [ list-arg-def ] ')'
					block
					'end'

	foreign-def :=	[ 'internal' ] 'foreign'
					[ type-decl ]
					Name
					'(' [ list-arg-def ] ')'

	method-name :=	Name [ '=' ] |
					binop | 
					'[' ']' [ '=' ]

	free-binop :=	'+' | '-' | '*' | '/' | '%' |
					'<' | '>' | '<=' | '>=' | '^' |
					'~' | '<~' | '==' | '~=' | 'and' | 'or'

	arg-def :=		type-decl Name

	block :=		{ statement }
					[ final-statement ]

	statement :=	do-stat |
					while-stat |
					for-stat |
					if-stat |
					assignment |
					call-stat

	final-statement :=	'break' | 'continue' | 'return' [ list-exp ]

	do-stat :=		'do' block 'end'

	while-stat :=	'while' exp [ 'as' Name ] 'do' block 'end'

	for-stat :=		'for' list-name 'in' list-exp 'do' block 'end'

	if-stat :=		'if' exp [ 'as' Name ] 'then' block
					{ 'elseif' exp [ 'as' Name ] 'then' block }
					[ 'else' block ]
					'end'

	call-stat :=	exp-start { exp-tail }

	assignment :=	list-left-value '=' list-exp

	left-value :=	exp-start { exp-tail } (and the last rule applied must not be call-arg)

	exp-start :=	[ type ':' ] Name [ call-arg ]

	exp :=			term { binop term }

	term :=			unary-op | typecast | basic-exp

	unary-op :=		( '-' | 'not' ) term

	typecast :=		basic-exp 'to' class-name

	basic-exp :=	( atomic-exp | exp-start | vec-cons | array-cons |
					table-cons | object-cons | '(' exp ')' )
					{ exp-tail }

	atomic-exp :=	Name | Number | String | 'true' | 'false' | 'nil'

	vec-cons :=		'<' list-basic-exp '>'

	array-cons :=	'[' [ list-exp ] ']'

	table-cons :=	'{' [ list-table-cons-pair ] '}'

	table-cons-pair :=	exp ':' exp

	object-cons :=	type [ array-cons | table-cons | with-cons | call-arg ]

	with-cons :=	'with' list-with-cons-pair 'end'

	with-cons-pair :=	Name '=' exp

	call-arg	:=	'(' [ list-exp ] ')' | String

	exp-tail :=		indexing | message-sending

	indexing :=		'[' list-exp ']'

	message-sending := 	'.' Name [ call-arg ]

It might seem a bit long, because I try to name rules a lot, to give a hint of the semantics behind. Also the left-value rule is fairly annoying to write explicitly because it has to translate the idea that a left-value cannot end in a call argument, it has to be an indexing or a single Name (that will map to methods `[]=` and `Name=`). But in concept, it is quite simple. Anyway, like I mentioned earlier, the parser will have a unified function for both left-values and call-statements, distinguishing between the two only by "is the top-most node a call with arguments or not?". Likewise, there are a few hidden left-factorings to do, but that will be a parser implementation issue.
